/*--------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
/*---------------------------- File graph.dox ------------------------------*/
/*--------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
/** \addtogroup area_graph */
/** \defgroup theory_graph Theory of graphs
 \ingroup area_graph

 \details The headers of `include/ADS/graph` realize the GRAPH of chapter 9 of
 the book and the algorithms of chapters 9, 11, 13, 14 and 15 that work on it,
 i.e., the visits, the shortest paths, the minimum spanning trees, the maximum
 flows and a few problems that reduce to them. This page collects what the
 headers take for granted: the definitions and the notation shared by all of
 them (see \ref theory_graph_notation), the representations and the costs of
 their operations (see \ref theory_graph_repr), and, for each family of
 algorithms, the property on which its correctness rests and the argument
 behind its bound (from \ref theory_graph_visits to \ref theory_graph_euler),
 and, beyond the book, the solutions kept up to date through the changes of
 the graph (see \ref theory_graph_dynamic), the assignment problem with
 its reoptimization (see \ref theory_graph_assign) and the policy that
 chooses between a repair and a computation from scratch (see
 \ref theory_graph_policy).
 The pseudocode of each algorithm is instead on the page of its header, in the
 comment of its function, together with the section of the book, or the paper,
 that it follows.

 \tableofcontents

 \section theory_graph_notation Definitions and notation

 A graph \f$ G = ( V , E ) \f$ has a set \f$ V \f$ of \f$ n \f$ nodes and a set
 \f$ E \f$ of \f$ m \f$ edges; an edge of an <em>oriented</em> graph is an
 ordered pair \f$ ( u , v ) \f$, which leaves \f$ u \f$ and enters \f$ v \f$,
 and an edge of an <em>unoriented</em> graph is an unordered pair
 \f$ [ u , v ] \f$, which the library stores as the two pairs \f$ ( u , v ) \f$
 and \f$ ( v , u ) \f$ (section 9.2 of the book). There are no loops
 \f$ ( u , u ) \f$, and hence \f$ 0 \le m \le n ( n - 1 ) \f$; a graph is said
 <em>sparse</em> when \f$ m \f$ is of the order of \f$ n \f$ and <em>dense</em>
 when it is of the order of \f$ n^2 \f$, which is the distinction that decides
 between the representations (see \ref theory_graph_repr). The nodes are named
 by the integers in \f$ [ 0 , \mathrm{bound}() ) \f$, a deleted node leaving
 its name unused, so that the vectors indexed by the nodes have
 \f$ \mathrm{bound}() \ge n \f$ places.

 <table>
 <caption>The symbols of the headers of the area</caption>
 <tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ \mathrm{adj}( u ) \f$</td>
     <td>the nodes \f$ v \f$ with \f$ ( u , v ) \in E \f$, the SET of the
         book; \f$ d( u ) = | \mathrm{adj}( u ) | \f$ is the (out)degree of
         \f$ u \f$, and \f$ \sum_u d( u ) = m \f$ (\f$ 2 m \f$ for an
         unoriented graph)</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ w( u , v ) \f$</td>
     <td>the weight, or cost, or length, of the edge \f$ ( u , v ) \f$, of
         the type <tt>W</tt> of the graph</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ \delta( r , v ) \f$, \f$ d[ v ] \f$</td>
     <td>the distance from \f$ r \f$ to \f$ v \f$ (the fewest edges, or the
         least total cost, of a path), and the value that an algorithm
         keeps for it; \f$ \infty \f$ (<tt>INF</tt>, <tt>infinity()</tt>)
         when \f$ v \f$ is not reachable</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ p[ v ] \f$, \f$ T[ v ] \f$</td>
     <td>the parent of \f$ v \f$ in a tree rooted at the source, as a vector
         of parents, <tt>NIL</tt> for the root and the nodes not
         reached</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ \mathit{dt}[ u ] \f$, \f$ \mathit{ft}[ u ] \f$</td>
     <td>the discovery and the finish times of \f$ u \f$ in a visit in
         depth, on one clock that each event advances by 1</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ c( u , v ) \f$, \f$ f( u , v ) \f$,
         \f$ c_f( u , v ) \f$</td>
     <td>the capacity of an edge of a network, the flow on it, and the
         residual capacity \f$ c( u , v ) - f( u , v ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ ( S , V \setminus S ) \f$</td>
     <td>a cut, the edges with one end in \f$ S \f$ and the other out of
         it</td></tr>
 </table>

 A <em>path</em> from \f$ u \f$ to \f$ v \f$ is a sequence of nodes
 \f$ u = x_0 , x_1 , \ldots , x_k = v \f$ with
 \f$ ( x_{i-1} , x_i ) \in E \f$; it is <em>simple</em> if its nodes are
 distinct, and it is a <em>cycle</em> if \f$ k \ge 1 \f$ and \f$ u = v \f$
 (\f$ k \ge 3 \f$ for an unoriented graph). A graph with no cycle is
 <em>acyclic</em> (a DAG if oriented, a forest if not, a free tree if it is
 also connected). The nodes reachable from one another, the orientation
 ignored, form the <em>connected components</em>, and those reachable from
 one another along the orientation the <em>strongly connected
 components</em>.

 \section theory_graph_repr Representations and their costs

 The library has five realizations of the GRAPH, which differ in the space and
 in the time of the operations. The algorithms of the area are written on the
 concept `Graph` of `Graph.h`, i.e., on `V()`, `adj( u )` and
 `forEachAdj( u , f )`, and their bounds hold on lists or vectors of adjacency,
 where the scan of \f$ \mathrm{adj}( u ) \f$ costs
 \f$ \Theta( d( u ) + 1 ) \f$.

 <table>
 <caption>The realizations of the GRAPH and the cost of their operations
          (sections 9.3 and 9.4 of the book)</caption>
 <tr><th>Realization</th><th>Space</th><th><tt>hasEdge( u , v )</tt></th>
     <th>scan of <tt>adj( u )</tt></th><th>insert or delete an edge</th></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>MatrixGraph</tt></td><td>\f$ \Theta( n^2 ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td><td>\f$ \Theta( n ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>ListGraph</tt></td><td>\f$ \Theta( n + m ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ O( d( u ) ) \f$</td><td>\f$ \Theta( d( u ) + 1 ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ O( d( u ) ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>ArrayGraph</tt>, <tt>StaticGraph</tt></td>
     <td>\f$ \Theta( n + m ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ O( d( u ) ) \f$ (\f$ O( \log d( u ) ) \f$ on <tt>StaticGraph</tt>,
         whose rows are sorted)</td>
     <td>\f$ \Theta( d( u ) + 1 ) \f$, one contiguous run</td>
     <td>none, the graph is static</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>PointerGraph</tt></td><td>\f$ \Theta( n + m ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ O( d( u ) ) \f$</td><td>\f$ \Theta( d( u ) + 1 ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ O( d( u ) ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>GridGraph</tt></td><td>one cost per cell, or none</td>
     <td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td><td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$, at most 8 cells</td>
     <td>none, the moves are computed</td></tr>
 </table>

 A matrix is usually preferable on the dense graphs and when `hasEdge()`
 dominates (e.g., `universalSink()` of `Properties.h` reads at most
 \f$ 3 ( n - 1 ) \f$ entries of it); on a sparse graph a visit costs
 \f$ \Theta( n^2 ) \f$ on the matrix against \f$ \Theta( n + m ) \f$ on the
 lists, since each row is read whole. Among the static realizations,
 `StaticGraph` stores each arc next to its weight and sorts the arcs of each
 node by head, so that a visit reads the memory forward (cf. `StaticGraph.h`);
 both are built from a list of edges by counting sort in
 \f$ \Theta( n + m ) \f$, since the keys are the names of the nodes, i.e.,
 integers in \f$ [ 0 , n ) \f$. The counting sort by tail of `ArrayGraph`
 is stable, and hence its rows keep the order in which the edges were
 given, while `StaticGraph` sorts them by head with a first pass on the
 heads; `GraphBuilder` of `GraphBuilder.h` collects the edges of a graph
 built one at a time and freezes it into either form at that cost.

 \section theory_graph_visits Visits

 The generic visit of section 9.5 (`genericVisit()` of `Visit.h`) keeps the
 reached nodes \f$ R \f$, the open ones \f$ Q \subseteq R \f$ and a tree
 \f$ B \f$: at each step it takes a node \f$ j \f$ of \f$ Q \f$ and either
 reaches a node \f$ k \in \mathrm{adj}( j ) \setminus R \f$, adding
 \f$ ( j , k ) \f$ to \f$ B \f$, or closes \f$ j \f$. A node enters
 \f$ R \f$ once, and a cursor in \f$ \mathrm{adj}( j ) \f$ never goes
 back; hence each node is taken \f$ O( 1 + d( j ) ) \f$ times, each edge is
 examined once from its tail, and a visit costs \f$ \Theta( n + m ) \f$
 time on lists. At the end \f$ R \f$ holds the nodes reachable from the
 root, and \f$ B \f$ is a tree that spans them, with one edge less than
 \f$ R \f$ (an invariant of the loop, checked at the level of the audits).
 Taking the oldest node of \f$ Q \f$ gives the visit in breadth, and taking
 the newest the visit in depth.

 <em>Lemma 1 (the visit in breadth).</em> With \f$ Q \f$ a queue,
 \f$ d[ v ] = d[ u ] + 1 \f$ set when \f$ v \f$ is reached from \f$ u \f$, and
 \f$ d[ r ] = 0 \f$, one has at the end
 \f[
   d[ v ] = \delta( r , v ) \quad \forall v \in V . \tag{1}
 \f]

 Indeed, the values of \f$ d \f$ in the queue never decrease from its head to
 its tail and differ by at most 1. A node at distance \f$ k \f$ is therefore
 dequeued before any node at distance \f$ k + 1 \f$ is reached, and by
 induction on \f$ k \f$ a node \f$ v \f$ with \f$ \delta( r , v ) = k + 1 \f$
 is first reached from a node \f$ u \f$ at distance \f$ k \f$, which gives (1).
 Hence `distance()` of `Visit.h` gives the shortest paths in edges in
 \f$ \Theta( n + m ) \f$, and its tree of the parents is a tree of shortest
 paths. The same argument holds with several sources at distance 0
 (`multiSourceDistance()` of `Paths.h` and `gridDistances()` of `Grid.h`).
 Also, `countShortestPaths()` of `Paths.h` adds to the count of \f$ v \f$ that
 of each \f$ u \f$ with \f$ d[ v ] = d[ u ] + 1 \f$, and by the same order of
 the levels the count is complete when \f$ v \f$ is dequeued.

 <em>Lemma 2 (the visit in depth).</em> In a visit in depth the intervals
 \f$ [ \mathit{dt}[ u ] , \mathit{ft}[ u ] ] \f$ of two nodes are either
 disjoint or nested, and \f$ v \f$ descends from \f$ u \f$ in the forest of
 the visit if and only if its interval lies in that of \f$ u \f$.
 Classifying an edge \f$ ( u , v ) \f$ when it is examined, as `DfsSchema` of
 `Visit.h` does, gives
 \f[
   \begin{array}{ll}
   \text{tree} & \mathit{dt}[ v ] = 0 , \cr
   \text{back} & \mathit{dt}[ u ] > \mathit{dt}[ v ] \text{ and }
                 \mathit{ft}[ v ] = 0 , \cr
   \text{forward} & \mathit{dt}[ u ] < \mathit{dt}[ v ] \text{ and }
                    \mathit{ft}[ v ] \ne 0 , \cr
   \text{cross} & \text{otherwise,}
   \end{array} \tag{2}
 \f]
 and an oriented graph has a cycle if and only if a visit finds a back edge.

 In fact, the nesting holds since the visit of \f$ v \f$ either starts and ends
 while \f$ u \f$ is on the stack, or does not start before \f$ u \f$ is
 finished. A back edge \f$ ( u , v ) \f$ closes a cycle with the path of the
 tree from \f$ v \f$ to \f$ u \f$. Conversely, if \f$ v \f$ is the first node
 of a cycle to be discovered, the other nodes of the cycle are reached before
 \f$ v \f$ is finished (the path of white nodes of Tarjan); the edge of the
 cycle that enters \f$ v \f$ is then examined while \f$ v \f$ is on the stack,
 i.e., it is a back edge. Note that on an unoriented graph each edge, met again
 from its other end, is a back edge in the sense of (2), and `hasCycle()`
 returns true as soon as the graph has an edge.

 Next, the finish times give a topological order. If the graph is acyclic,
 each edge \f$ ( u , v ) \f$ has
 \f[
   \mathit{ft}[ v ] < \mathit{ft}[ u ] , \tag{3}
 \f]
 since by (2) it is a tree, forward or cross edge, and \f$ v \f$ is finished
 before \f$ u \f$ in all three cases; hence the nodes by decreasing finish
 time are in a topological order (`topsort()`). The algorithm of Kahn
 (`topsortKahn()`) reaches the same goal by removing, at each step, a node with
 no entering edge, which an acyclic graph always has. With the counts of the
 entering edges kept in a vector it costs \f$ \Theta( n + m ) \f$, and it
 stops with fewer than \f$ n \f$ nodes if and only if a cycle remains. The
 topological order is unique if and only if the set of the sources never
 holds two nodes (`uniqueTopologicalOrder()` of `Properties.h`), i.e., if and
 only if consecutive nodes of the order are joined by an edge.

 <em>Lemma 3 (Kosaraju).</em> Let \f$ C \f$ and \f$ C' \f$ be two strongly
 connected components, and \f$ \mathit{ft}( C ) \f$ the largest finish time of
 a node of \f$ C \f$ in a visit in depth of \f$ G \f$. If an edge goes from
 \f$ C \f$ to \f$ C' \f$, then \f$ \mathit{ft}( C ) > \mathit{ft}( C' ) \f$
 (Theorem 9.1 of the book).

 Therefore, a visit of the transposed graph \f$ G^T \f$ started from the
 node of largest finish time stays in its component, since an edge that
 leaves it in \f$ G^T \f$ can only enter a component already visited; the
 next visit, from the largest finish time left, does the same. Thus, `scc()`
 of `Visit.h` costs two visits, each \f$ \Theta( n + m ) \f$, and the copy
 of the transposed graph. The graph of the components is acyclic, and
 `unilaterallyConnected()` tests whether its topological order is unique.

 One visit in depth suffices, if the components are recognized as it
 finishes them (Tarjan, beyond the book). The <em>root</em> of a component
 \f$ C \f$ is its node \f$ r_C \f$ discovered first; by Lemma 2 every node
 of \f$ C \f$ is a descendant of \f$ r_C \f$, since all of them are
 undiscovered when \f$ r_C \f$ is discovered and reachable from it. The
 visit pushes each node it discovers on a stack \f$ S \f$, and keeps
 \f$ \mathit{low}[ u ] \f$, the least \f$ \mathit{dt} \f$ of a node of
 \f$ S \f$ that the subtree of \f$ u \f$ reaches by its tree edges and one
 more edge, \f$ \mathit{dt}[ u ] \f$ itself included.

 <em>Lemma 4 (the roots of Tarjan).</em> When \f$ u \f$ finishes,
 \f$ u \f$ is a root if and only if
 \f$ \mathit{low}[ u ] = \mathit{dt}[ u ] \f$; the nodes above \f$ u \f$
 on \f$ S \f$ are then exactly the nodes of its component.

 Indeed, if \f$ u \f$ is not a root, the root \f$ r \f$ of its component
 is a proper ancestor of \f$ u \f$, still open and on \f$ S \f$, and a
 path from \f$ u \f$ to \f$ r \f$ leaves the subtree of \f$ u \f$ by an
 edge \f$ ( x , y ) \f$ whose head \f$ y \f$ reaches \f$ r \f$; the
 component of \f$ y \f$ is that of \f$ r \f$, which is not closed, so
 that \f$ y \f$ is on \f$ S \f$ with \f$ \mathit{dt}[ y ] <
 \mathit{dt}[ u ] \f$, and \f$ \mathit{low}[ u ] < \mathit{dt}[ u ] \f$.
 Conversely, if \f$ \mathit{low}[ u ] < \mathit{dt}[ u ] \f$ the subtree
 of \f$ u \f$ reaches a node \f$ y \f$ of \f$ S \f$ discovered before
 \f$ u \f$; the nodes of \f$ S \f$ discovered before \f$ u \f$ are in
 components still open, whose roots are ancestors of \f$ u \f$, so that
 the root of the component of \f$ y \f$ reaches \f$ u \f$ by the tree and
 is reached from \f$ u \f$ through \f$ y \f$: \f$ u \f$ is in a component
 opened before it, and is not a root. Finally, when the root \f$ u \f$
 finishes, the nodes above it on \f$ S \f$ are its descendants not yet
 closed, and a descendant in another component would have a root between
 \f$ u \f$ and itself, which has closed it before. The test of
 \f$ y \in S \f$ is that \f$ y \f$ has no component yet, so that the
 visit costs \f$ \Theta( n + m ) \f$ (`sccTarjan()` of `Visit.h`): each
 node is pushed and popped once, and each edge examined once. When a
 component closes, every component that it reaches is closed already;
 hence the components close in the reverse of a topological order of their
 graph, and `sccTarjan()` numbers them backwards, as `scc()` numbers them:
 an edge from \f$ C \f$ to \f$ C' \ne C \f$ gives \f$ C < C' \f$.
 The recursion is kept on a stack of the pairs (node, next edge), and the
 adjacencies are read once into compressed rows, so that the cost holds on
 every model of `Graph` and a path of \f$ 10^6 \f$ nodes does not overflow
 the stack of the calls.

 The visit of Tarjan keeps three integers per node (\f$ \mathit{dt} \f$,
 \f$ \mathit{low} \f$ and the component); Pearce keeps one, and a bit
 (`sccPearce()`). The integer \f$ \mathit{rindex}[ u ] \f$ starts as the
 order of discovery and takes, instead of \f$ \mathit{low}[ u ] \f$, every
 smaller \f$ \mathit{rindex} \f$ met, the bit recording whether it took
 one; when \f$ u \f$ closes its component, its nodes take the number
 \f$ c \f$ of the component, which counts down from \f$ N - 1 \f$, and
 the counter \f$ i \f$ of the discoveries goes back by one per node
 closed.

 <em>Lemma 5 (one integer per node).</em> The numbers of the open nodes are
 at most \f$ c + 1 \f$, and those of the closed ones at least
 \f$ c + 1 \f$.

 Indeed, an open node has a number below \f$ i \f$, and \f$ i - 1 \f$
 counts the open nodes, which are at most the \f$ c + 1 \f$ names of the
 \f$ N \f$ not closed, the closed ones being \f$ N - 1 - c \f$; the
 closed nodes have the numbers \f$ c + 1 , \ldots , N - 1 \f$ of their
 components. Hence a comparison of the numbers alone never lets a closed
 node lower an open one, as the test \f$ y \in S \f$ of Lemma 4 does, and
 a node of \f$ S \f$ belongs to the component of the root \f$ u \f$ if
 and only if its number is at least \f$ \mathit{rindex}[ u ] \f$; the
 visit makes the same choices as that of Tarjan, in the same time, with
 \f$ N \f$ words and \f$ N \f$ bits besides the two stacks, and the
 components numbered \f$ \mathit{rindex}[ u ] - c \f$ at the end come out
 in the order of `sccTarjan()`.

 \section theory_graph_sp Shortest paths

 With a cost \f$ w( u , v ) \f$ on each edge and a source \f$ r \f$, a tree
 \f$ T \f$ rooted in \f$ r \f$, with \f$ d[ v ] \f$ the cost of its path from
 \f$ r \f$ to \f$ v \f$, is a tree of shortest paths if and only if (theorem
 of Bellman, Theorem 11.1 of the book)
 \f[
   d[ v ] = d[ u ] + w( u , v ) \quad \forall ( u , v ) \in T , \qquad
   d[ u ] + w( u , v ) \ge d[ v ] \quad \forall ( u , v ) \in E , \tag{4}
 \f]
 provided that no cycle of negative cost is reachable from \f$ r \f$ (with
 one, no shortest path exists). The prototype of section 11.2
 (`shortestPaths()` of `ShortestPaths.h`) starts from \f$ d[ r ] = 0 \f$ and
 \f$ d[ v ] = \infty \f$ elsewhere and, while an edge violates (4), sets
 \f$ d[ v ] = d[ u ] + w( u , v ) \f$ and makes \f$ u \f$ the parent of
 \f$ v \f$; a structure \f$ S \f$ holds the nodes whose edges have to be
 checked again. Each \f$ d[ v ] \f$ is at all times the cost of some path
 from \f$ r \f$, and it only decreases; hence, when \f$ S \f$ is empty, (4)
 holds and \f$ d \f$ is optimal. The kind of \f$ S \f$ decides how many
 times a node is extracted, and therefore the cost.

 <table>
 <caption>The structure \f$ S \f$ of the prototype and its cost</caption>
 <tr><th>\f$ S \f$</th><th>Algorithm</th><th>Time</th>
     <th>Negative costs</th></tr>
 <tr><td>priority queue on an unsorted list</td><td>Dijkstra</td>
     <td>\f$ O( n^2 ) \f$</td><td>correct, a node possibly extracted more
     than once</td></tr>
 <tr><td>binary heap</td><td>Johnson</td><td>\f$ O( m \log n ) \f$</td>
     <td>as above</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Fibonacci heap</td><td>Fredman and Tarjan</td>
     <td>\f$ O( m + n \log n ) \f$</td><td>as above</td></tr>
 <tr><td>queue</td><td>Bellman, Ford and Moore</td>
     <td>\f$ O( n m ) \f$</td><td>a negative cycle detected</td></tr>
 <tr><td>stack, deque</td><td>Pape and D'Esopo (deque)</td>
     <td>exponential in the worst case</td><td>no negative cycle
     allowed</td></tr>
 </table>

 With nonnegative costs and \f$ S \f$ a priority queue on \f$ d \f$, the
 node \f$ u \f$ extracted has \f$ d[ u ] = \delta( r , u ) \f$: any other
 path to \f$ u \f$ leaves the extracted nodes through a node of \f$ S \f$
 of priority at least \f$ d[ u ] \f$, and goes on with costs that are not
 negative. Hence each node is extracted once and each edge is examined once,
 and the time is that of \f$ n \f$ extractions and of at most \f$ m \f$
 decreases of a priority, i.e., \f$ n \cdot O( n ) + m \cdot O( 1 ) \f$
 on the unsorted list, \f$ ( n + m ) \cdot O( \log n ) \f$ on the binary
 heap and \f$ n \cdot O( \log n ) + m \cdot O( 1 ) \f$ amortized on the
 Fibonacci heap. With a queue, the nodes extracted in the \f$ k \f$-th
 round (those that entered \f$ S \f$ in the round before) have their final
 distance if one of their shortest paths has at most \f$ k \f$ edges. A
 shortest path has at most \f$ n - 1 \f$ edges; hence each node leaves the
 queue at most \f$ n \f$ times, the time is \f$ O( n m ) \f$, and a node
 extracted more than \f$ n \f$ times proves that a negative cycle is
 reachable (exercise 11.2). On an acyclic graph one can do better, whatever
 the signs of the costs: the scan of the nodes in topological order relaxes
 the edges of each node once, after all the edges that enter it, and (4)
 holds in \f$ O( n + m ) \f$ (`dagShortestPaths()`; the longest paths of
 `dagLongestPaths()` of `Paths.h` follow the same recurrence with the maximum).

 Between all the pairs of nodes, with \f$ d^k[ u ][ v ] \f$ the least cost
 of a path from \f$ u \f$ to \f$ v \f$ whose inner nodes are among the first
 \f$ k \f$, one has (section 13.5)
 \f[
   d^k[ u ][ v ] = \min\{ d^{k-1}[ u ][ v ] ,
                          d^{k-1}[ u ][ k ] + d^{k-1}[ k ][ v ] \} ,
   \tag{5}
 \f]
 since such a path either avoids the node \f$ k \f$ or passes through it
 once (no cycle being negative); `floydWarshall()` of `AllPairsShortestPaths.h`
 fills (5) in place, in \f$ \Theta( n^3 ) \f$ time and
 \f$ \Theta( n^2 ) \f$ space, and a negative cycle shows as
 \f$ d[ u ][ u ] < 0 \f$. After the insertion of an edge \f$ ( a , b ) \f$
 a path that improves uses it once, and the update
 \f$ d[ u ][ v ] \gets \min\{ d[ u ][ v ] , d[ u ][ a ] + w + d[ b ][ v ] \}
 \f$ costs \f$ O( n^2 ) \f$ (`addEdge()`); a deletion has no such update.

 \section theory_graph_grids Graphs on grid maps

 A grid map of \f$ R \times C \f$ cells, each free or blocked, is the graph
 whose nodes are the free cells and whose edges are the moves from a cell to
 the free cells at its four sides or, with eight moves, also at its four
 corners (the tiles and the octiles in the terms of Yap). The programs of
 best-first search on such maps do not build this graph. The maps of the
 benchmarks of Sturtevant are text files, a header with the height and the
 width followed by one character per cell, and the fast searchers keep them
 as one flat array of the cells in the order of the rows, one byte or one bit
 per cell and often with a border of blocked cells, name the cell
 \f$ ( r , c ) \f$ by its index \f$ r C + c \f$, and compute the neighbours by
 the offsets \f$ \pm 1 \f$, \f$ \pm C \f$ and \f$ \pm C \pm 1 \f$. Jump point
 search (Harabor and Grastien) is built on this access: it prunes the
 neighbours of a cell by the direction of the move that reached it and jumps
 along the rows and the columns, reading only whether a cell is free, and its
 improved version reads a word of cells of a row at once from a bitmap of the
 map (with a transposed copy for the columns), or looks the lengths of the
 jumps up in tables computed in advance. Lists of adjacency would hold up to
 \f$ 8 n \f$ arcs that the offsets give for free, and would add a read of
 memory to each neighbour.

 `GridGraph` of `GridGraph.h` keeps the map as a vector of costs, a cell
 blocked if its cost is not positive (with one byte per cell for the maps of
 the benchmarks, and no vector at all for a free map of unit costs), and names
 the cell \f$ ( r , c ) \f$ by \f$ r C + c \f$ with no border, so that the
 names are those of the map; the row and the column of a node, one division,
 replace the border in the tests on the bounds. With \f$ \gamma( v ) \f$ the
 cost of the cell \f$ v \f$, a move from \f$ u \f$ into \f$ v \f$ costs
 \f$ \gamma( v ) \, a \f$ to a side and \f$ \gamma( v ) \, b \f$ to a corner
 when the cell entered pays (the default of `MoveCost`), and
 \f$ \gamma( u ) \, a \f$ and \f$ \gamma( u ) \, b \f$ when the cell left
 pays, \f$ a = 1 \f$ and \f$ b = \sqrt{2} \f$ in the benchmarks (the octile
 costs); a move to a corner either may cut it, or needs one of its two sides
 free, or needs both (the rule of the benchmarks since 2012). All three rules
 are symmetric in the two ends of a move, and hence, under both models, the
 graph is unoriented when the free cells cost the same and oriented
 otherwise. With \f$ w_1( u , v ) \in \{ a , b \} \f$ the cost of a move on
 a map of unit costs, a path \f$ x_0 , x_1 , \ldots , x_k \f$ costs
 \f$ \sum_{i = 1}^{k} \gamma( x_i ) \, w_1( x_{i-1} , x_i ) \f$ when the
 cell entered pays and
 \f$ \sum_{i = 1}^{k} \gamma( x_{i-1} ) \, w_1( x_{i-1} , x_i ) \f$ when the
 cell left pays, i.e., as much as the reversed path in the first model; hence
 the distances from \f$ s \f$ in the second model are the distances to
 \f$ s \f$ in the first. With four moves every \f$ w_1 \f$ is \f$ a \f$ and
 the two costs of a path from \f$ s \f$ to \f$ t \f$ differ by
 \f$ a ( \gamma( s ) - \gamma( t ) ) \f$, the same for all such paths, so
 that the two models have the same shortest paths; with eight moves the
 weight \f$ b \f$ of a corner falls on a different cell in the two models,
 and their shortest paths may differ.

 <em>Lemma 6 (the octile distance).</em> On a grid with no blocked cell and
 eight moves of costs \f$ a \f$ and \f$ b \f$ with \f$ a \le b \le 2 a \f$,
 let \f$ M \f$ and \f$ \mu \f$ be the larger and the smaller of
 \f$ | r - r' | \f$ and \f$ | c - c' | \f$; the least cost of a path from
 \f$ ( r , c ) \f$ to \f$ ( r' , c' ) \f$ is
 \f[
   h = a ( M - \mu ) + b \, \mu , \tag{6}
 \f]
 and on any grid of the same moves, with costs
 \f$ \gamma( v ) \ge \gamma_{\mathrm{min}} \f$ of the cells, some of them
 blocked and either the cell entered or the cell left paying for a move,
 \f$ \gamma_{\mathrm{min}} h \f$ is a consistent lower bound on the cost to
 \f$ ( r' , c' ) \f$.

 Indeed, a path of \f$ x \f$ moves to a side and \f$ y \f$ to a corner has
 \f$ x + y \ge M \f$, since each move brings the larger gap down by at most
 1, and \f$ x + 2 y \ge M + \mu \f$, since a move to a corner brings the sum
 of the two gaps down by at most 2 and one to a side by at most 1; the
 least of \f$ a x + b y \f$ on these constraints is at one of the vertices
 \f$ ( M - \mu , \mu ) \f$, \f$ ( M + \mu , 0 ) \f$ and \f$ ( 0 , M ) \f$,
 and \f$ a \le b \le 2 a \f$ makes the first one the least, which a path of
 \f$ \mu \f$ diagonal moves and \f$ M - \mu \f$ straight ones attains. Hence
 (6) is the distance of a graph, which satisfies the triangle inequality,
 i.e., \f$ h( u ) \le w_1( u , v ) + h( v ) \f$ for each move of cost
 \f$ w_1( u , v ) \in \{ a , b \} \f$ on the free grid; since a move of the
 weighted grid costs
 \f$ w( u , v ) = \gamma( x ) \, w_1( u , v ) \ge \gamma_{\mathrm{min}} \,
 w_1( u , v ) \f$, \f$ x \f$ the cell that pays (\f$ v \f$ or \f$ u \f$),
 and the blocked cells only remove moves, the bound
 \f$ \gamma_{\mathrm{min}} h \f$ is consistent, and so admissible, on every
 such grid. With \f$ b = a \f$ (the moves of the king) (6) is the distance of
 Chebyshev \f$ a M \f$, and with four moves the same argument with
 \f$ y = 0 \f$ gives that of Manhattan \f$ a ( M + \mu ) \f$
 (`gridHeuristic()`, which takes \f$ \min\{ b , 2 a \} \f$ as the cost of a
 corner). The A* of Hart, Nilsson and Raphael with a consistent bound never
 takes a node out of its queue twice, and with (6) on a map with few
 obstacles it usually examines far fewer cells than Dijkstra's algorithm,
 with the same bound in the worst case.

 The visits of the library run on `GridGraph` as on any other `Graph`, since
 the free cells are its nodes and the names of the cells its range
 \f$ [ 0 , R C ) \f$; a visit in breadth gives the distances in moves
 (Lemma 1), which are the costs of the shortest paths when the free cells
 cost the same and the moves are four, and Dijkstra's algorithm the
 distances of (4) otherwise. The node \f$ r C + c \f$ is an int, and hence
 the grid has at most \f$ 2^{31} - 1 \f$ cells; the neighbours of
 \f$ u \f$ are computed from its row and its column before any offset is
 added, so that no sum leaves \f$ [ 0 , R C ) \f$.

 The costs of the cells change by `setCost()`, which tells each change as
 the event `Changed< Value , int , C >` through the subject policy of the
 grid (no code with `NoObservers`). It keeps \f$ n \f$, the least cost
 \f$ \gamma_{\mathrm{min}} \f$ of a free cell and the number \f$ m \f$ of
 the free cells of that cost: taking the cell out and putting it back with
 its new cost keeps all three in \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ time, but when the cell
 was the last one of the least cost and its new cost is larger, which
 needs a new count of the grid; the grid is unoriented exactly when
 \f$ m = n \f$. An arc \f$ ( x , v ) \f$ exists when \f$ x \f$, \f$ v \f$
 and, for a corner, its sides allow it, and it weighs the cost of
 \f$ v \f$ or of \f$ x \f$; hence a change of the cell \f$ u \f$ touches
 only the arcs into \f$ u \f$ (the cell entered pays) or out of \f$ u \f$
 (the cell left pays) when \f$ u \f$ stays free, and only arcs whose head is
 \f$ u \f$ or a free cell at a move of \f$ u \f$ when it is blocked or
 freed, since \f$ u \f$ is a side of the corners between two of its
 neighbours. `forEachAffected()` gives these heads, at most nine, and for a
 batch the union of its calls on the coalesced events, read after the
 batch, holds every head of a changed arc: an arc that changed has one of
 its at most four cells changed, and a case analysis on which one (the
 cell that pays, an end blocked or freed, or a side) finds an event whose
 call gives the head. An incremental search (LPA*, D* Lite) thus repairs
 the right-hand sides of those heads only. The heuristic of the grid reads
 \f$ \gamma_{\mathrm{min}} \f$, which may change; such a search takes a
 lower bound of the costs that the changes may give instead.

 \section theory_graph_mst Minimum spanning trees

 On an unoriented connected graph a spanning tree has \f$ n - 1 \f$ edges,
 and a minimum one has the least total weight. Both algorithms of
 `MinimumSpanningTree.h` rest on the property of the cuts (Theorem 14.1 of the
 book), which reads as follows: if \f$ A \f$ is contained in a minimum
 spanning tree and no edge of \f$ A \f$ crosses the cut
 \f$ ( S , V \setminus S ) \f$, then
 \f[
   A \cup \{ e \} \text{ is contained in a minimum spanning tree, }
   e \text{ a lightest edge across } ( S , V \setminus S ) . \tag{7}
 \f]
 Indeed, in a minimum tree that does not contain \f$ e \f$ the path between
 the ends of \f$ e \f$ crosses the cut on some edge \f$ e' \f$ with
 \f$ w( e ) \le w( e' ) \f$, and swapping \f$ e' \f$ for \f$ e \f$ gives
 another spanning tree that is no heavier.

 `kruskal()` takes the edges by increasing weight and keeps one if its ends are
 in two components of the forest built so far, i.e., if it is a lightest
 edge across the cut around one of them. The sort costs
 \f$ O( m \log n ) \f$ (or \f$ O( m + n + k ) \f$ by counting, with integer
 weights in \f$ [ 0 , k ) \f$), and with an MFSET with the rank and the
 compression of the paths the rest costs \f$ O( m \, \alpha( m , n ) ) \f$,
 \f$ \alpha \f$ the inverse of the function of Ackermann, which is at most
 4 in practice. `prim()` grows one tree from \f$ r \f$, the cut being the tree
 itself, and keeps the nodes out of it in a priority queue by the weight of
 their lightest edge to it; this gives the same three bounds as the shortest
 paths (\f$ O( n^2 ) \f$, \f$ O( m \log n ) \f$ and
 \f$ O( m + n \log n ) \f$), the list being usually preferable on the dense
 graphs. Dually, a spanning forest is minimum if and only if no edge out of
 it weighs less than the heaviest edge of the path of the forest between its
 ends (the property of the cycles), and `isMinimumSpanningForest()` checks it
 in \f$ O( m n ) \f$.

 \section theory_graph_flow Flows, cuts and matchings

 A network has a source \f$ s \f$, a sink \f$ t \f$ and a capacity
 \f$ c( u , v ) \ge 0 \f$ on each edge; a flow satisfies
 \f$ 0 \le f( u , v ) \le c( u , v ) \f$ and is conserved at every node but
 \f$ s \f$ and \f$ t \f$, and its value \f$ | f | \f$ is the net flow out of
 \f$ s \f$. `FlowNetwork` of `MaxFlow.h` keeps for each edge a twin in the
 opposite direction, with \f$ f( v , u ) = - f( u , v ) \f$, so that the
 residual network, made of the edges with \f$ c_f( u , v ) > 0 \f$, is at hand.

 <em>Theorem 7 (maximum flow, minimum cut).</em> The following are equivalent:
 (i) \f$ f \f$ is a maximum flow; (ii) the residual network has no path from
 \f$ s \f$ to \f$ t \f$ (an augmenting path); (iii)
 \f$ | f | = c( S , V \setminus S ) \f$ for some cut with \f$ s \in S \f$ and
 \f$ t \notin S \f$ (Theorem 15.1 of the book).

 A flow is bounded by the capacity of any cut, and hence (iii) gives (i);
 (i) gives (ii), since an augmenting path would increase the flow by its
 least residual capacity. For (ii) to (iii), the nodes \f$ S \f$ reachable
 from \f$ s \f$ in the residual network give a cut whose edges are
 saturated and whose reverse edges carry no flow (`minCut()`).

 The algorithms augment along augmenting paths until (ii) holds, and differ in
 the path chosen. With integer capacities each augmentation adds at least 1;
 hence any path (`FORD_FULKERSON`, by a visit in depth) gives
 \f$ O( ( n + m ) | f^* | ) \f$, a bound that depends on the values of the
 capacities and may be poor when they are large with respect to \f$ n \f$ and
 \f$ m \f$. A shortest path in edges (`EDMONDS_KARP`, by a visit in breadth)
 never decreases the distances of the residual network. Each edge is then the
 critical edge of an augmentation at most \f$ n / 2 \f$ times, since between
 two such times the distance of its tail grows by 2; there are therefore
 \f$ O( n m ) \f$ augmentations of \f$ O( m ) \f$ each, i.e.,
 \f$ O( n m^2 ) \f$. `DINIC` sends at once a blocking flow along all the
 shortest paths of the levels of a visit in breadth, and each such phase
 increases the distance of \f$ t \f$. At most \f$ n \f$ phases of
 \f$ O( n m ) \f$ give \f$ O( n^2 m ) \f$, and the bound falls to
 \f$ O( m \sqrt{n} ) \f$ on the networks of unit capacities (Even and Tarjan),
 e.g., those of the bipartite matching; in practice the phases are usually far
 fewer than \f$ n \f$.

 The push-relabel of Goldberg and Tarjan (`PUSH_RELABEL`, beyond the book,
 in `PushRelabel.h`) gives up the conservation while it runs: it keeps a
 <em>preflow</em>, in
 which each node but \f$ s \f$ has an excess
 \f$ e( v ) \ge 0 \f$ of entering flow over leaving flow, and a label
 \f$ d( v ) \f$ with \f$ d( t ) = 0 \f$ and \f$ d( u ) \le d( v ) + 1 \f$
 on every edge with \f$ c_f( u , v ) > 0 \f$, so that \f$ d( v ) \f$ is a
 lower bound of the distance in edges from \f$ v \f$ to \f$ t \f$ in the
 residual network. It starts by saturating the edges that leave
 \f$ s \f$, and then a node \f$ u \f$ with an excess either pushes it on an
 edge with \f$ c_f( u , v ) > 0 \f$ and \f$ d( v ) = d( u ) - 1 \f$, or,
 if there is none, raises \f$ d( u ) \f$ to the least
 \f$ d( v ) + 1 \f$ over its residual edges, which keeps the labels valid.
 A label never decreases and stays below \f$ 2 n \f$, since a node with an
 excess has a residual path back to \f$ s \f$; hence there are
 \f$ O( n^2 ) \f$ relabelings and \f$ O( n m ) \f$ pushes that saturate
 their edge, and taking the node of the highest label bounds the others by
 \f$ O( n^2 \sqrt{m} ) \f$ (Cheriyan and Maheshwari). When no node with an
 excess has a label below \f$ n \f$ the preflow is maximum, since a
 residual path from \f$ s \f$ to \f$ t \f$ would descend from
 \f$ d( s ) = n \f$ to \f$ 0 \f$ by at most 1 per edge in fewer than
 \f$ n \f$ edges; the excesses left are then sent back to \f$ s \f$ by the
 same pushes with the labels of the distances from \f$ s \f$. The bound
 says little of the speed, which comes from two heuristics of Cherkassky and
 Goldberg: the <em>global relabeling</em>, a visit in breadth backwards from
 \f$ t \f$ that sets each label to its distance, every \f$ O( n + m ) \f$
 work of the relabelings; and the <em>gap</em>, which, when no node is left
 with some label \f$ k \f$, puts out of the game every node of label
 above \f$ k \f$, since none of them reaches \f$ t \f$ any longer. Three
 choices matter as much on the sparse networks, where the work is mostly
 the first labeling: each labeling stops after the level of the last node
 with an excess, the nodes not reached getting the next level but one,
 still a lower bound of their distance (the lazy update of the HIPR of
 Cherkassky and Goldberg, in its version 4); the second phase labels only
 the nodes left at the top, the side of \f$ s \f$ of the cut, as the
 Preflow of LEMON does; and a node scans its edges once, pushing to the
 lower neighbours and taking the least label of the others, which is its
 new label if an excess is left. The first labeling takes a node by a
 conditional write instead of a branch, its tests on the labels and on
 the residual capacities being hard to predict.
 `FlowNetwork` adds a layout for the visits: the head of each edge is
 stored next to its number in the list of its tail, and the residual
 capacities in an array of their own, so that a visit reads 8 bytes of an
 edge, and none of an edge whose head it has already labeled.

 Every way above starts from the flow there is. When a capacity falls below
 the flow of its edge \f$ ( u , v ) \f$, `FlowNetwork::setCapacity()` cuts
 the flow to the new capacity and leaves an excess at \f$ u \f$ and a
 deficit at \f$ v \f$ of the difference (the reparametrization of the
 dynamic cuts of Kohli and Torr); the next `maxFlow()` first sends each
 excess along a residual path to \f$ s \f$, to \f$ t \f$ or to a deficit,
 and then fills each deficit from \f$ s \f$ or \f$ t \f$, both of which
 give and take any amount. Such paths exist: the flow before the cut decomposes
 into paths from \f$ s \f$ to \f$ t \f$ and cycles, and those through
 \f$ ( u , v ) \f$ give, backwards from \f$ u \f$ and forwards from
 \f$ v \f$, the residual paths needed. The result is a flow again, from
 which the maximum is reached by the augmentations or by the push-relabel.
 The two differ here: the augmenting paths of `DINIC` start from the flow
 and stop at the first visit from \f$ s \f$ that misses \f$ t \f$, which
 explores the side of \f$ s \f$ alone; the push-relabel first makes the
 same visit when there is a flow, and stops too if \f$ t \f$ is not
 reached, but otherwise it labels the network by a visit from \f$ t \f$
 before it pushes the excesses, which it takes where `setCapacity()` left
 them. Both repair a
 change for less than a computation from scratch, and on a large network
 with few paths to find `DINIC` is usually the cheaper; `MaxFlowObserver`
 takes it unless told otherwise.

 The flow found is integral when the capacities are (the integrality theorem),
 and this makes three reductions exact. The edge-disjoint paths of
 `edgeDisjointPaths()` of `FlowApplications.h` are as many as the maximum flow
 with unit capacities (the theorem of Menger). The b-matching of
 `bipartiteBMatching()` of `Matching.h` is a maximum flow from a source joined
 to the left side to a sink joined from the right one. Finally, a circulation
 with lower bounds (`feasibleCirculation()`) exists if and only if, after the
 lower bounds are sent, a maximum flow from a new source to a new sink balances
 the excess left at each node.

 A matching of a graph that is not bipartite is beyond the flows: a
 network has no orientation that turns its paths into the alternating
 paths of a general graph. Let \f$ M \f$ be a matching; a node is
 <em>free</em> if no edge of \f$ M \f$ touches it, and a path is
 <em>alternating</em> if its edges are in turn out of \f$ M \f$ and in it,
 <em>augmenting</em> if it is alternating and joins two free nodes; its
 symmetric difference with \f$ M \f$ is a matching with one edge more.

 <em>Lemma 8 (Berge).</em> A matching is of largest size if and only if no
 path augments it.

 Indeed, if \f$ | M' | > | M | \f$, the symmetric difference of the two is
 made of paths and of even cycles in which the edges of \f$ M \f$ and of
 \f$ M' \f$ alternate, and one of its paths has more edges of
 \f$ M' \f$ than of \f$ M \f$: it augments \f$ M \f$.

 A search for such a path from a free node \f$ r \f$ grows an alternating
 tree: the nodes at an even distance from \f$ r \f$ along the tree are
 <em>even</em>, the others <em>odd</em>, each odd node having its mate as
 its only child. An edge from an even node to a free node not in the tree
 ends an augmenting path, and one to a matched node not in the tree adds it
 and its mate; an edge between two even nodes closes an odd cycle, a
 <em>blossom</em>, whose node nearest to \f$ r \f$, its <em>base</em>, is
 even and is the only node of the cycle matched outside it, if at all.

 <em>Lemma 9 (Edmonds).</em> Let \f$ G / C \f$ be the graph in which the
 blossom \f$ C \f$ is shrunk to one node, and \f$ M / C \f$ the matching
 left. Then \f$ M \f$ has an augmenting path in \f$ G \f$ if and only if
 \f$ M / C \f$ has one in \f$ G / C \f$.

 Indeed, a path of \f$ G / C \f$ through the shrunk node enters it by an
 edge to some node of \f$ C \f$, and goes on from the base: one of the two
 ways around the cycle from that node to the base has an even number of
 edges and alternates, and lifts the path to \f$ G \f$. Conversely, an
 augmenting path of \f$ G \f$ either misses \f$ C \f$ or can be cut at
 its first node in \f$ C \f$ after the stem from \f$ r \f$ to the base
 is exchanged with the matching, which keeps the sizes and makes the base
 free; the part before the cut is then augmenting in \f$ G / C \f$.

 So the search shrinks each blossom it closes, every node of the cycle
 becoming even (the odd ones are then searched too), and stops at the first
 augmenting path, which it lifts through the blossoms, or with a tree in
 which every edge from an even node goes to an odd one. Such a tree is
 <em>Hungarian</em>: no augmenting path can ever use its nodes, since its
 odd nodes cover all the edges of its even nodes, and so they are left out
 of every later search, a free root that failed never being reached again.
 `maxCardinalityMatching()` of `BlossomMatching.h` keeps the blossoms as
 the sets of a union-find with the union by rank and the halving of the
 paths, the representative of a set storing its base; the base common to
 two even nodes is found by walking from both towards the root one step at
 a time, which costs no more than twice the cycle shrunk. For each odd node
 that a blossom makes even it records the edge \f$ ( a , b ) \f$ that closed
 the blossom, its own side first, as the labels of Gabow do: the path from
 such a node to the root goes back along the path of \f$ a \f$ down to
 \f$ a \f$ and then follows that of \f$ b \f$, and the augmentation
 rematches the two parts in turn, each stopping at the node whose mate it
 has changed already. A search examines each edge of its tree at most twice
 and merges each node once, i.e., it costs
 \f$ O( m \, \alpha( m , n ) ) \f$ besides the walks, which are paid by
 the merges; at most \f$ n / 2 \f$ searches succeed and each node is the
 root of at most one that fails, so that the whole costs
 \f$ O( n m \, \alpha( m , n ) ) \f$, i.e., \f$ O( n m ) \f$ for every
 practical \f$ n \f$ and within the \f$ O( n^3 ) \f$ of the
 implementation of Gabow. Gabow and Tarjan remove the factor
 \f$ \alpha \f$ with a union-find for the trees of the search, and
 Micali and Vazirani reach \f$ O( m \sqrt{n} ) \f$ by augmenting along a
 maximal set of the shortest disjoint paths at once, as Hopcroft and Karp
 do on bipartite graphs, at the price of a much more delicate search.

 With a weight \f$ w( e ) \f$ on each edge, a matching of largest total
 weight is a linear program over the matchings, whose polytope Edmonds
 described: the vectors \f$ x \ge 0 \f$ with
 \f$ x( \delta( v ) ) \le 1 \f$ at each node and
 \f$ x( E( B ) ) \le ( | B | - 1 ) / 2 \f$ on each set \f$ B \f$ of an
 odd number of nodes. Its dual has a variable \f$ y_v \ge 0 \f$ per node
 and \f$ z_B \ge 0 \f$ per odd set, and asks
 \f[
   \pi( e ) = y_u + y_v - 2 w( e ) + 2 \sum_{B \ni u , v} z_B \ge 0
   \quad \forall e = [ u , v ] \in E ,
 \f]
 with the duals doubled so that integer weights keep them integer.

 <em>Theorem 10 (Edmonds).</em> A matching \f$ M \f$ is of largest weight
 if there are duals as above such that (i) \f$ \pi( e ) = 0 \f$ on every
 edge of \f$ M \f$, (ii) \f$ y_v = 0 \f$ at every free node, and (iii)
 every odd set with \f$ z_B > 0 \f$ holds \f$ ( | B | - 1 ) / 2 \f$ edges
 of \f$ M \f$.

 Indeed, the conditions are the complementary slackness of the two
 programs: for any matching \f$ M' \f$, summing \f$ \pi( e ) \ge 0 \f$
 over its edges gives \f$ 2 w( M' ) \le \sum_v y_v + \sum_B z_B ( | B | -
 1 ) \f$, a bound that (i), (ii) and (iii) turn into an equality for
 \f$ M \f$. Among the matchings of largest size, condition (ii) is dropped
 and the \f$ y_v \f$ may be shifted by a constant, the free nodes having
 the least dual.

 `maxWeightMatching()` of `BlossomMatching.h` keeps the three conditions but
 (ii), which it approaches: every \f$ y_v \f$ starts at the largest weight
 and the matching empty, and the free nodes, whose duals are always equal
 and the least, are the roots of the trees grown on the edges of slack 0
 alone, the blossoms with \f$ z_B > 0 \f$ being the odd sets of (iii).
 When no tight edge extends a tree, the duals change by \f$ \delta \f$:
 down on the outer nodes \f$ S \f$ and up on the inner ones \f$ T \f$,
 up on the outer blossoms and down on the inner ones, which keeps the slack
 of every edge of a tree, and \f$ \delta \f$ is the least of the free
 duals (then (ii) holds and the matching is optimal), of the slacks from
 \f$ S \f$ to a node out of the trees, of half the slacks between two
 outer blossoms, and of the duals of the inner blossoms, one of which is
 then expanded. Each change thus makes an edge tight or a blossom
 expandable, and a stage, which ends at the first augmentation, has
 \f$ O( n ) \f$ of them, since every event of the four kinds either grows
 the trees, or shrinks or expands a blossom, all of which happen
 \f$ O( n ) \f$ times before an augmentation. The least slack towards
 each outer blossom is kept per blossom and merged when blossoms are
 shrunk, so that a change of the duals costs \f$ O( n ) \f$, and a stage
 \f$ O( n^2 + m ) \f$; with at most \f$ n / 2 \f$ augmentations, the whole
 costs \f$ O( n^3 ) \f$, the bound of Gabow and Lawler that Galil surveys.
 With integer weights the slack between two outer nodes is even, so that
 \f$ \delta \f$ stays integer, and at the end the duals certify the
 optimum by Theorem 10, which the audits check. Gabow reaches
 \f$ O( n m + n^2 \log n ) \f$ with mergeable heaps of the slacks and
 the nearest common ancestors of the blossoms, a structure whose constants
 pay off only on large sparse graphs.

 \section theory_graph_euler Eulerian trails and other properties

 A trail is a path that uses each edge exactly once, and an Eulerian cycle
 a closed trail. Let the excess \f$ x( u ) \f$ of a node of an oriented
 graph be the number of the edges leaving it minus that of the edges
 entering it, and call a node of an unoriented graph odd when its degree
 is.

 <em>Lemma 11 (Euler).</em> Let the edges of \f$ G \f$ lie in one
 component, the orientation ignored. An oriented \f$ G \f$ has an
 Eulerian cycle if and only if \f$ x( u ) = 0 \f$ for every node, and an
 open trail if and only if one node \f$ s \f$ has \f$ x( s ) = 1 \f$, one
 node \f$ t \f$ has \f$ x( t ) = - 1 \f$ and the others have excess 0,
 the trail going from \f$ s \f$ to \f$ t \f$; an unoriented \f$ G \f$
 has an Eulerian cycle if and only if no node is odd, and an open trail if
 and only if exactly two nodes are odd, the trail joining them. Without the
 connection, there is no trail.

 Indeed, a trail enters and leaves each node it passes through, which
 gives the conditions; and, under them, a walk along unused edges from
 \f$ s \f$ (from any node with an edge, if all are balanced) can stop only
 at \f$ t \f$ (at its start), since every other node it enters has an
 unused edge to leave by. The algorithm of Hierholzer (`eulerianTrail()`
 and `eulerianCycle()` of `EulerianCycle.h`) keeps the walk on a stack: a
 node with an unused edge pushes its other end, and a node with none is
 popped and written, so that the closed walks found from the nodes of the
 walk are spliced into it, and the trail comes out backwards. Each edge is
 followed once, and a cursor per node never goes back; an unoriented edge
 is listed at both its ends with one mark of use, the cursor skipping the
 edges used from the other end, so that the walk costs
 \f$ O( n + m ) \f$. The walk uses every edge reachable from its start,
 and, under the conditions on the excesses, all of them when they lie in
 one component, since a balanced component is strongly connected (with the
 edge \f$ ( t , s ) \f$ added in the open case); a walk of fewer than
 \f$ m \f$ edges thus tells that the edges are not connected, which needs
 no other visit.

 Several other headers decide properties by the visits above, with the bound of
 a visit unless their comments say otherwise. A free tree has \f$ m = n - 1 \f$
 edges and one component (`isTree()`). A graph is bipartite if and only if it
 has no odd cycle (the theorem of Koenig), i.e., if and only if no edge of a
 visit in breadth joins two nodes of levels of the same parity
 (`twoColoring()`). In a free tree the node farthest from any node ends a
 longest path, and the node farthest from it ends the other (the double sweep
 of `treeDiameter()`). The triangles are counted in \f$ O( m \sqrt{m} ) \f$ by
 orienting each edge towards the end of larger degree, which leaves each node
 with \f$ O( \sqrt{m} ) \f$ out-neighbours (`countTriangles()`). Finally, the
 algorithm of Johnson lists the simple cycles through a node in
 \f$ O( ( n + m ) ( c + 1 ) ) \f$ for \f$ c \f$ cycles (`simpleCycles()` of
 `Paths.h`), since it keeps the search from entering a branch with no cycle at
 its end; the backtracking of chapter 16 may instead spend exponential time
 between two cycles.

 \section theory_graph_dynamic Changes of the graph and reoptimization

 Beyond the book, `Observable.h` wraps a graph of any model and turns each of
 its changes into an <em>event</em>, i.e., the insertion or the deletion of a
 node, the insertion or the deletion of an edge with its weight, or the change
 of a weight from \f$ w_0 \f$ to \f$ w_1 \f$, which it sends to the
 <em>observers</em> attached to it, each of them with a mask \f$ M_o \f$ of
 the kinds of event that it wants (the pattern Observer of Gamma, Helm,
 Johnson and Vlissides). With \f$ M = \bigcup_o M_o \f$ the union of the
 masks, a change of kind \f$ k \notin M \f$ builds no event at all, and costs
 what it costs on the graph plus one test; a change that is listened to costs
 \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ per observer that wants it. The changes made inside a
 <em>batch</em> are collected instead of being sent, and the closing of the
 batch replaces those on each edge by their net effect: with \f$ s_0 \f$ the
 state of the edge before its first event and \f$ s_1 \f$ the state after its
 last one, both either \f$ \bot \f$ (absent) or a weight,
 \f[
   ( s_0 , s_1 ) \mapsto \left\{ \begin{array}{ll}
     \emptyset & \text{if } s_0 = s_1 , \cr
     \mathrm{EdgeInserted}( s_1 ) & \text{if } s_0 = \bot \ne s_1 , \cr
     \mathrm{EdgeDeleted}( s_0 ) & \text{if } s_0 \ne \bot = s_1 , \cr
     \mathrm{WeightChanged}( s_0 , s_1 ) & \text{otherwise} ,
   \end{array} \right. \tag{8}
 \f]
 the weights compared by `==` when their type has it (never equal otherwise),
 and a node both inserted and deleted within the batch vanishes with all its
 edges. The batch is sent as one span, made of the nodes inserted, then of the
 net events of the edges in the order of their first event, then of the nodes
 deleted.

 <em>Lemma 12 (coalescing).</em> Let \f$ B \f$ be the sequence of the events
 of a batch on a graph \f$ G \f$, and \f$ C \f$ the sequence that (8) gives.
 The graph obtained by replaying \f$ C \f$ on \f$ G \f$ is the one obtained by
 replaying \f$ B \f$, and \f$ |C| \le |B| \f$.

 Indeed, the state of an edge after \f$ B \f$ depends on its last event only,
 and (8) gives an event that leads from \f$ s_0 \f$ to \f$ s_1 \f$, or none if
 the two are equal; the nodes inserted come first, so that every edge of
 \f$ C \f$ finds its ends, and the nodes deleted come last, after the
 deletions of their edges, which the wrapper sends before the deletion of a
 node. Each event of \f$ C \f$ stands for at least one of \f$ B \f$; a hash
 table on the edges builds \f$ C \f$ in \f$ O( |B| ) \f$ expected time
 (`coalesce()`), and an observer whose mask lacks some kinds of \f$ C \f$ gets
 a filtered copy, in \f$ O( |C| ) \f$.

 An observer of `Reoptimization.h` keeps the solution of a problem on the
 graph together with a <em>certificate</em> of its optimality, i.e., data that
 prove the solution optimal by conditions that each touch a single edge. It
 queues the events and works at the next query (the lazy flush): the queue
 \f$ Q \f$ is first coalesced by (8) into \f$ P \f$, and if
 \f$ |P| > \rho \max( m , 1 ) \f$, \f$ \rho \f$ the ratio of
 `setRecomputeRatio()` (\f$ 1 / 4 \f$ by default), the solution is computed
 from scratch, since a repair that touches a large part of the graph costs as
 much and is slower by a constant. Otherwise each event of \f$ P \f$ is tested
 against the certificate of the previous solution and found
 <em>harmless</em>, <em>repairable</em> or <em>fatal</em>; the outcome of the
 query (`outcome()`) is then from scratch (0) if one event is fatal, by a
 repair (1) if one is repairable, and by the certificate (2) if all are
 harmless, in which case nothing is computed.

 <em>Lemma 13 (the batch test).</em> If each condition of the certificate
 involves the weight of a single edge, and each event of \f$ P \f$ is tested
 on the certificate of the solution before \f$ P \f$, then the events of
 \f$ P \f$ found harmless leave the solution optimal all together.

 Indeed, the events of \f$ P \f$ are on distinct edges by Lemma 12, so that
 each condition of the certificate is touched by at most one of them, and a
 harmless event keeps true the conditions that it touches; the conditions
 that no event touches stay as they were.

 For the shortest paths from \f$ r \f$ (`ShortestPathsObserver`) the
 certificate is the tree \f$ T \f$ with the distances \f$ d \f$, i.e., the
 reduced costs \f$ \bar{w}( u , v ) = d[ u ] + w( u , v ) - d[ v ] \f$, zero
 on the edges of \f$ T \f$ and nonnegative on all the edges by (4). A new
 weight \f$ w' \f$ of an edge \f$ ( u , v ) \notin T \f$ with
 \f$ d[ u ] + w' \ge d[ v ] \f$, or the deletion of an edge out of \f$ T \f$,
 is harmless. A new weight with \f$ d[ u ] + w' < d[ v ] \f$, or a decrease
 of an edge of \f$ T \f$, is repairable: \f$ d \f$ is still the cost of real
 paths, an upper bound of the distances, and the prototype of section 11.2
 started with \f$ S = \{ u \} \f$ for the tails \f$ u \f$ of all such edges,
 instead of \f$ S = \{ r \} \f$, makes it exact (the algorithm of Ramalingam
 and Reps for the decreases), in
 \f$ O( ( n_\delta + m_\delta ) \log n_\delta ) \f$ with nonnegative weights,
 \f$ n_\delta \f$ and \f$ m_\delta \f$ the nodes whose distance falls and
 their edges. An increase or the deletion of an edge \f$ ( u , v ) \f$ of
 \f$ T \f$ is repairable too, at a higher price: the distances of the nodes
 of the subtree of \f$ T \f$ under \f$ v \f$ may grow, and the certificate
 gives no bound of them, while those of the other nodes are still the costs
 of their paths in \f$ T \f$, which no raised edge touches.

 <em>Lemma 14 (the increases).</em> Let \f$ A \f$ be the union of the
 subtrees of \f$ T \f$ under the heads of the edges of \f$ T \f$ raised or
 deleted by \f$ P \f$. Set \f$ d[ x ] = \infty \f$ for \f$ x \in A \f$, then
 \f[
   d[ x ] = \min_{( u , x ) \in E} \{ d[ u ] + w( u , x ) \} \quad
   \forall x \in A , \tag{9}
 \f]
 on the weights after \f$ P \f$, and run the prototype with \f$ S \f$ made
 of the nodes of \f$ A \f$ with \f$ d[ x ] < \infty \f$ and of the tails of
 the edges that violate (4); at the end \f$ d \f$ holds the distances after
 \f$ P \f$ and \f$ T \f$ is a tree of shortest paths.

 Indeed, a node out of \f$ A \f$ keeps its path in \f$ T \f$, whose edges
 were not raised, so that \f$ d \f$ stays an upper bound of its distance;
 the nodes of \f$ A \f$ get \f$ \infty \f$ or, by (9), the cost of a path,
 again an upper bound. The prototype keeps the upper bounds, and it stops
 when no edge violates (4): the edges between two nodes out of \f$ A \f$
 whose weight did not fall satisfied (4) before \f$ P \f$ and still do, the
 edges that enter \f$ A \f$ satisfy it after (9), the edges out of the nodes
 whose \f$ d \f$ changes are examined when these leave \f$ S \f$, and the
 lowered ones are in \f$ S \f$ by their tails. Hence (4) holds with
 \f$ d[ r ] = 0 \f$, and \f$ d \f$ is at most each distance and at least it.

 The repair costs the scan of the edges that enter \f$ A \f$, which an
 oriented graph keeps in lists of its own from the first increase on (the
 graphs of the library give only the edges that leave a node), plus the
 prototype on the nodes whose distance changes; it is the algorithm of
 Ramalingam and Reps with the subtree of \f$ T \f$ in place of the graph of
 all the shortest paths, which is the variant that the experiments of
 Bauer and Wagner on batches of changes found the most robust, since it
 needs no count of the tight edges and no order among the nodes of
 \f$ A \f$. The subtrees are found by lists of the children of \f$ T \f$,
 updated whenever a parent changes, so that a batch that touches few nodes
 costs no \f$ \Theta( n ) \f$; when \f$ |A| > n / 2 \f$ the tree is computed
 from scratch, which then costs about as much. Only the threshold \f$ \rho \f$
 of the events and a negative cycle are still fatal.

 For the minimum spanning forest \f$ F \f$ (`SpanningForestObserver`) the
 certificate is the pair of conditions of optimality of section 14.3, i.e.,
 with \f$ F( u , v ) \f$ the path of \f$ F \f$ between \f$ u \f$ and
 \f$ v \f$, the property of the cycles and the property of the cuts (7):
 \f[
   w( f ) \ge \max_{e \in F( u , v )} w( e ) \quad
   \forall f = [ u , v ] \notin F , \qquad
   w( e ) \le w( \rho( e ) ) \quad \forall e \in F , \tag{10}
 \f]
 \f$ \rho( e ) \f$ the <em>replacement</em> of \f$ e \f$, the lightest edge
 out of \f$ F \f$ whose path holds \f$ e \f$ (the sensitivity analysis of
 Tarjan). The forest is kept by a link-cut tree (see
 \ref theory_tree_dynamic), with a node in the middle of each of its edges
 that carries its weight, so that the maximum of (10) on a path, and the
 edge that attains it, take \f$ O( \log n ) \f$ amortized time, and an edge
 enters or leaves \f$ F \f$ by two links or two cuts, in the same time. The
 events of \f$ P \f$ are applied one at a time, each keeping \f$ F \f$
 minimum for the graph after it. Raising an edge out of \f$ F \f$, lowering
 one of \f$ F \f$ and deleting one out of \f$ F \f$ keep (10) and change
 nothing but a weight. Lowering or inserting an edge \f$ f = [ u , v ] \f$
 out of \f$ F \f$ is harmless if \f$ w( f ) \f$ is at least the maximum on
 \f$ F( u , v ) \f$; if it is less, the edge of that maximum leaves and
 \f$ f \f$ enters, by (7) on the cut that the removal of the former leaves;
 if \f$ u \f$ and \f$ v \f$ are in two trees, \f$ f \f$ enters. Raising or
 deleting an edge \f$ e \f$ of \f$ F \f$ splits its tree in two, and the
 lightest edge between the two parts (\f$ e \f$ itself among them, with its
 new weight, unless deleted) joins them again, by (7); no such edge leaves
 the forest split (a bridge deleted). That edge is sought by two searches
 run in turn, a few steps each, the first one that ends giving it: two
 visits of \f$ F \f$ from the ends of \f$ e \f$, one node of each in turn,
 which stop when one of them ends and then scan the edges at the nodes of
 the smaller part, in time proportional to that part and to its edges;
 and a scan of the edges out of \f$ F \f$ by increasing weight, kept in a
 balanced tree, the first one whose ends have two distinct roots in the
 link-cut tree being the answer. With \f$ s \f$ the nodes of the smaller
 part and \f$ \bar{d} \f$ their mean degree, the first costs about
 \f$ s \bar{d} \f$, and the second, when the ends of the edges out of
 \f$ F \f$ are spread at random, about \f$ n / s \f$ edges of
 \f$ O( \log n ) \f$ each; together, at most twice the cheaper of the two,
 i.e., about \f$ \sqrt{ n \bar{d} \log n } \f$ in that model, though
 \f$ \Theta( m ) \f$ in the worst case (two large parts with no edge
 between them). A batch with many cuts is joined again at once, instead: the
 edges raised or deleted leave \f$ F \f$, the edges lowered or inserted
 off \f$ F \f$ are set aside, and the trees left are joined by the
 algorithm of Kruskal on the trees contracted, whose edges are those
 between two trees, found in the lists of the edges at the nodes out of
 the largest tree and taken by blocks of the lightest ones (the
 filter-Kruskal of Osipov, Sanders and Singler); the edges set aside are
 then taken one at a time, by the cycles. This is correct since the
 forest so joined is minimum for the graph without the edges set aside
 (the remaining edges of \f$ F \f$ are lightest across the cuts that
 their removal leaves, as they were), and each edge taken by its cycle
 afterwards keeps it minimum; the batch costs \f$ O( n ) \f$ plus the
 edges between two trees, against \f$ O( m \log m ) \f$ for Kruskal from
 scratch, and the observer takes it when the cuts times the mean work of
 its replacements so far exceed \f$ n \f$ plus the edges off
 \f$ F \f$. The structures that answer this
 too in polylogarithmic time (the levels of Holm, de Lichtenberg and
 Thorup) keep \f$ O( \log n ) \f$ copies of the forest, and they lose in
 practice to the simpler scans on the graphs that are not adversarial (the
 experiments surveyed by Hanauer, Henzinger and Schulz). Every event costs
 \f$ O( \log n ) \f$ amortized but the raises and the deletions of the edges
 of \f$ F \f$, with no structure built again; the binary lifting that a
 static forest would use costs \f$ O( n \log n ) \f$ at each change of its
 shape.

 For the maximum flow from \f$ s \f$ to \f$ t \f$ (`MaxFlowObserver`) the
 certificate is the side \f$ S \f$ of a minimum cut with
 \f$ | f | = c( S , V \setminus S ) \f$, by (iii) of Theorem 7. A new
 capacity \f$ c' \f$ of \f$ ( u , v ) \f$ with \f$ c' \ge f( u , v ) \f$ is
 harmless unless \f$ ( u , v ) \f$ leaves \f$ S \f$ and grows: the flow stays
 feasible, and the capacity of the cut neither falls below \f$ | f | \f$ (an
 edge of the cut is saturated, so that lowering it below its flow is caught
 by the other test) nor rises. If \f$ ( u , v ) \f$ crosses the cut and
 grows, the flow is still feasible and the maximum is sought from it, not
 from zero; if \f$ c' < f( u , v ) \f$ the flow is cut to \f$ c' \f$, its
 excess and its deficit are routed as above, and the maximum is sought from
 the flow so obtained. Only the threshold of the events is fatal.

 <table>
 <caption>The tests of the observers on a change of the edge
 \f$ ( u , v ) \f$, and the work of each outcome</caption>
 <tr><th>Observer</th><th>Harmless</th><th>Repairable</th><th>Fatal</th></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>ShortestPathsObserver</tt></td><td>off \f$ T \f$ with
     \f$ d[ u ] + w' \ge d[ v ] \f$, \f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td><td>an improving
     edge, the prototype from its tail; an edge of \f$ T \f$ raised or
     deleted, (9) on its subtree and the prototype</td><td>a subtree of
     more than \f$ n / 2 \f$ nodes</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>SpanningForestObserver</tt></td><td>(10) still true,
     \f$ O( \log n ) \f$</td><td>a swap on a cycle, \f$ O( \log n ) \f$;
     an edge of \f$ F \f$ raised or deleted, the lightest edge out of the
     smaller part</td><td>more than \f$ \rho m \f$ events</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>MaxFlowObserver</tt></td><td>\f$ c' \ge f( u , v ) \f$ off
     the cut, \f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td><td>an edge of the cut raised, the
     maximum from \f$ f \f$; \f$ c' < f( u , v ) \f$, the excess and the
     deficit routed first</td><td>more than \f$ \rho m \f$
     events</td></tr>
 </table>

 \section theory_graph_assign The assignment and its reoptimization

 Beyond the book, `Assignment.h` solves the assignment problem: \f$ n \f$
 rows and \f$ n \f$ columns, a cost \f$ c( i , j ) \f$ for each pair (or
 none, i.e., \f$ \infty \f$), and a perfect matching of the rows to the
 columns of least total cost. Its dual gives a potential \f$ u_i \f$ to each
 row and \f$ v_j \f$ to each column, and maximizes
 \f$ \sum_i u_i + \sum_j v_j \f$ subject to
 \f[
   \bar{c}( i , j ) = c( i , j ) - u_i - v_j \ge 0
   \quad \forall ( i , j ) , \tag{11}
 \f]
 the <em>reduced costs</em>; a perfect matching \f$ M \f$ whose pairs have
 \f$ \bar{c}( i , j ) = 0 \f$ is then optimal, since its cost is
 \f$ \sum_i u_i + \sum_j v_j \f$, a lower bound of the cost of any perfect
 matching by (11) (the complementary slackness, as in the Hungarian method
 of Kuhn). The algorithm of the shortest augmenting paths (Jonker and
 Volgenant, in the form of Crouse) keeps (11) and the zeros on a partial
 matching, and adds one row at a time: from a free row \f$ i_0 \f$ it
 finds, by the algorithm of Dijkstra on the reduced costs of the complete
 bipartite graph (alternating between a column reached and the row matched
 to it), a shortest path to a free column, of length \f$ \mu \f$; it then
 raises \f$ u \f$ and lowers \f$ v \f$ along the tree of the search by
 \f$ \mu - \delta_j \f$, \f$ \delta_j \f$ the distance of each column
 \f$ j \f$ scanned, which keeps (11) and makes the edges of the path
 tight, and flips the pairs along the path. On the dense graph a search
 costs \f$ O( n^2 ) \f$, with an array in place of the heap, and \f$ n \f$
 of them give \f$ O( n^3 ) \f$; the potentials start from
 \f$ u_i = \min_j c( i , j ) \f$ and \f$ v = 0 \f$, which satisfy (11)
 whatever the signs of the costs.

 <em>Lemma 15 (the change of a row).</em> Let \f$ ( u , v , M ) \f$ satisfy
 (11) with the pairs of \f$ M \f$ tight, and let the costs of the row
 \f$ i \f$ change. Removing the pair of \f$ i \f$ from \f$ M \f$ and setting
 \f[
   u_i = \min_j \{ c( i , j ) - v_j \} \tag{12}
 \f]
 gives again a triple that satisfies (11) with the pairs tight, with one
 free row, so that one augmentation, \f$ O( n^2 ) \f$, gives an optimal
 assignment for the new costs; the same holds for a column \f$ j \f$, with
 \f$ v_j = \min_i \{ c( i , j ) - u_i \} \f$, and for any set of rows and
 columns, one augmentation for each pair removed.

 Indeed, (12) makes the reduced costs of the row \f$ i \f$ nonnegative,
 and those of the other rows did not change; the pairs left in \f$ M \f$
 are outside the row \f$ i \f$, so that they stay tight. A column \f$ j \f$
 is the same with the roles exchanged, and when the rows are set first the
 minimum of the columns sees their new potentials. This is the incremental
 assignment of Toroslu and Üçoluk (a row and a column added to a solved
 problem, solved by one augmentation; Volgenant remarks that it is a step
 of the method of Jonker and Volgenant) and the dynamic Hungarian algorithm
 of Mills-Tettey, Stentz and Dias for the costs that change, here with the
 shortest augmenting path in place of the labels of the Hungarian method.
 A change of a single cost \f$ c( i , j ) \f$ off \f$ M \f$ that leaves
 \f$ \bar{c}( i , j ) \ge 0 \f$ (a raise, or a fall that the duals allow)
 changes nothing, in \f$ O( 1 ) \f$; any other frees the row \f$ i \f$. With
 \f$ k \f$ rows and columns changed, the repair costs \f$ O( k n^2 ) \f$
 against the \f$ O( n^3 ) \f$ of a solution from scratch, which
 `Assignment::solve()` chooses when \f$ 2 k > n \f$.

 \section theory_graph_policy The recompute policy of the observers

 Beyond the book, each observer of `Reoptimization.h` and the `Assignment`
 decide between a repair and a computation from scratch by a recompute
 policy (`ReoptPolicy` of `Modification.h`), an object of their own with
 three entry points: `scratch()`, asked before the repair with the net
 events \f$ k \f$ and the size \f$ s \f$ of the instance (the edges, or
 the \f$ n^2 \f$ costs of the assignment), `abandon()`, asked with the work
 \f$ w \f$ of the repair against the work \f$ \ell \f$ of a computation
 from scratch, and `record()`, told the cause of the outcome afterwards.
 The work is a count of elementary operations that the observer has at
 hand (the nodes whose distance is lost, the steps of the replacements of
 the forest, the rows and the columns freed), never a time, and the policy
 therefore decides the same on every machine. The default `RatioPolicy`
 answers \f$ k > \rho \max( s , 1 ) \f$ to the first question and
 \f$ w > \alpha \ell \f$ (or more than \f$ \beta > 0 \f$ extractions of
 one node beyond its first, for the shortest paths) to the second, with
 \f$ \rho = 1 / 4 \f$, \f$ \alpha = 1 / 2 \f$ and \f$ \beta = 0 \f$
 (off); the forest sets \f$ \alpha = 1 \f$ in its own object. The calls
 are the following: the shortest paths ask `abandon()` with
 \f$ w = |A| \f$, the subtrees lost of Lemma 14, against \f$ \ell = n \f$,
 the nodes that a computation from scratch settles at most, when \f$ A \f$
 is not empty, and again at each extraction of a node beyond its first; the
 forest asks it with \f$ w \f$ the cuts times the mean work of its
 replacements so far, against \f$ \ell = n + |E \setminus F| \f$, the work
 of the join of the trees, and on a yes it joins the trees in one pass
 instead of replacing the cuts one at a time; the assignment asks it with
 \f$ w = |D_r| + |D_c| \f$ against \f$ \ell = n \f$; the maximum flow
 asks only `scratch()`, and the observer of the assignment asks neither
 (the events cost it only through the rows and the columns they touch).

 <em>Lemma 16 (the default policy).</em> With `RatioPolicy` and its
 default values, each observer computes from scratch at the same queries,
 and repairs or certifies at the others, as with the fixed thresholds of
 \ref theory_graph_dynamic and \ref theory_graph_assign
 (\f$ |P| > \rho \max( m , 1 ) \f$, \f$ 2 |A| > n \f$ for the shortest
 paths, the cuts times the mean work above \f$ n + |E \setminus F| \f$ for
 the batch of the forest, \f$ 2 ( |D_r| + |D_c| ) > n \f$ for the
 assignment), and therefore it gives the same solutions with the same
 counts of the outcomes.

 Indeed, the first test is the same expression of floating point as
 before. For the others, \f$ w > \ell / 2 \f$ with \f$ w \f$ and
 \f$ \ell \f$ integers below \f$ 2^{53} \f$ is \f$ 2 w > \ell \f$, since
 \f$ \ell / 2 \f$ is exact in floating point, and \f$ w > 1 \cdot \ell \f$
 is \f$ w > \ell \f$. The question of the shortest paths is skipped only
 when \f$ A = \emptyset \f$, where \f$ 2 |A| > n \f$ is false anyway, and
 the question at each extraction beyond the first answers no, since
 \f$ \beta = 0 \f$ switches off its second part and its first part is the
 test \f$ 2 |A| > n \f$ already answered no; the detection of a negative
 cycle (more than \f$ n \f$ extractions) comes before it, as before. At the
 close of a batch `scratch()` is asked with `closing` set, and answers no
 unless `early`, so that the subject sends the events as before. The
 policy keeps no state (`record()` does nothing), the questions come in
 the order of the old tests, and the causes refine the outcomes
 (`Reopt::outcomeOf()`), so that each count of `count()` is the one of
 before. Finally, the periodic sum of the floating total of the forest
 changes the total only, not the outcomes, and is skipped for exact
 weights.

 <em>Lemma 17 (the fallback).</em> At any query of an observer, and at
 any point of its repair, computing the solution from scratch in place of
 the repair gives an optimal solution of the graph of now, whatever the
 policy answered, and also after a `Rebuilt` that made it drop its queue.

 Indeed, a computation from scratch reads only the graph, or a copy of it
 brought up to date by the events of the query before the computation,
 and it overwrites every part of the state that the repair may have
 touched. The shortest paths compute the tree by the algorithm of the
 book on the graph, and rebuild the lists of the children of \f$ T \f$
 and the marks of \f$ A \f$ (the counts of the extractions are reset by
 the repair before it returns, and the lists of the entering edges are
 dropped, to be built again at their next use). The forest computes
 Kruskal on its copy of the edges, to which the loop of the query has
 applied every event of \f$ P \f$ before the computation, or, after
 `Rebuilt`, on a copy made again from the graph. The maximum flow builds
 its network again from the graph. The assignment computes from the costs,
 into which the events of \f$ P \f$ were written, or, after `Rebuilt`,
 every row read again from the graph. The solution is then optimal by the
 correctness of the algorithm of the book, and the certificate of the
 next query is the one of that solution. A policy can therefore only make
 an observer slower or faster, never wrong: the price of a bad answer is
 a computation from scratch where a repair would have cost less, or the
 reverse.

 The total weight of the forest is kept by additions and subtractions as
 the weights of its edges change, and on floating weights it drifts: a
 plain sum after \f$ k \f$ terms has an error of the order of
 \f$ k \varepsilon \f$ times the largest magnitude added (Higham, chapter
 4), which an edge raised to \f$ 10^{17} \f$ and back makes larger than the
 total of the other edges. The policy bounds it in two ways: (i) the total
 is summed again from \f$ F \f$ after \f$ \mathit{period} \cdot |F| \f$
 changes (\f$ \mathit{period} = 1 \f$ by default), at an amortized cost of
 \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ per change, so that \f$ k \f$ stays of the order of
 \f$ |F| \f$; (ii) with `compensated`, the sum is that of Neumaier
 (`NeumaierSum`), whose error is of the order of \f$ \varepsilon \f$
 times the sum of the absolute values of the terms, independently of
 \f$ k \f$. At the level of the audits the observer compares its total with
 the sum over \f$ F \f$ within \f$ 10^{-9} \f$ times the sum of the absolute
 weights of \f$ F \f$; the default periodic sum may of course fail this
 test on adversarial magnitudes between two sums, and the audit is there
 to catch it in the tests.

 A batch on a wrapper whose observers listen to every kind is collected
 whole, and when it holds only changes of edges (insertions, deletions and
 changes of weights, each with the old value) it can be undone by them.

 <em>Lemma 18 (the rollback of a batch).</em> Let a batch collect the
 events \f$ e_1 , \ldots , e_k \f$ of changes of edges only, every kind
 being listened to. Applying the inverses \f$ e_k^{-1} , \ldots ,
 e_1^{-1} \f$ under a mute gives back the weights of every edge (and its
 absence), and the observers, told nothing, are as before the batch.

 Indeed, every change of an edge through the wrapper emits its event,
 since every kind is wanted, and the batch collects it; the change after
 \f$ e_1 , \ldots , e_{i-1} \f$ is undone by \f$ e_i^{-1} \f$, which
 restores the state of its edge before \f$ e_i \f$, and by induction from
 \f$ i = k \f$ down to \f$ 1 \f$ every edge gets its state before the
 batch. The inverses emit nothing under the mute, and the events of the
 batch are dropped, so that no observer receives anything. The graph is
 the same up to the order of the edges in its lists, which the algorithms
 of the observers read only to break ties. A node inserted or deleted has
 no such inverse (the numbers of the nodes are not reused), and a batch
 that holds one is sent to the observers instead, as on a structure that
 cannot be undone; `UndoLog` undoes in the same way the changes of edges
 sent outside a batch, through `apply()`, and forgets its records at a
 change of a node.

 \section theory_graph_morin Morin's representations and exercises

 The `AdjacencyMatrix` and the `AdjacencyLists` of chapter 12 of Morin's
 book are `MatrixGraph` and `ListGraph`, whose `adj( u )` is its
 `outEdges()` and `inAdj( u )` its `inEdges()`, the column of \f$ u \f$ in
 \f$ \Theta( n ) \f$ on the matrix and a scan of all the lists in
 \f$ O( n + m ) \f$ on the lists. The matrix of incidence of exercise 12.2,
 \f$ n \times m \f$ with \f$ -1 \f$ at the source and \f$ +1 \f$ at the
 target of each edge, is `IncidenceGraph`: kept by columns, it takes
 \f$ n m \f$ bytes, `insertEdge()` appends a column in \f$ O( n ) \f$
 amortized, `hasEdge( i , j )` reads the row \f$ i \f$ and, at each
 \f$ -1 \f$, the entry \f$ j \f$, \f$ O( m ) \f$, `deleteEdge()` copies the
 last column over the one found, \f$ O( m + n ) \f$, and `adj( i )` and
 `inAdj( i )` search the column of each edge of \f$ i \f$ for its other end,
 \f$ O( m + n \deg( i ) ) \f$; no operation beats the other two
 representations, and its use is the algebra of the graph [see
 `incidence()`], not the visits.

 <em>Lemma 19 (the universal sink).</em> A universal sink, a node with an
 edge from each of the other \f$ n - 1 \f$ nodes and no edge out, is found or
 shown not to exist with at most \f$ 3 ( n - 1 ) \f$ calls of
 `hasEdge()`, \f$ O( n ) \f$ time on the adjacency matrix (exercise 12.10).

 Indeed, the test of \f$ ( c , j ) \f$ rules out \f$ c \f$ if the edge is
 there, since a sink has no edge out, and \f$ j \f$ otherwise, since the
 sink has an edge from every node: a scan of \f$ j = 1 , \ldots , n - 1 \f$
 that moves \f$ c \f$ to \f$ j \f$ at each edge found leaves one candidate,
 \f$ n - 1 \f$ tests, and \f$ 2 ( n - 1 ) \f$ more check it (`universalSink()`
 of `Properties.h`, on a matrix or on any graph with `hasEdge()`).

 The other exercises of the chapter are solved by the visits of
 `Visit.h`, each in \f$ O( n + m ) \f$: an unoriented graph is connected if
 `cc()` gives one component, whose numbers are the labels of the components
 (exercises 12.4 and 12.5); the parents that `distance()` gives from one
 node of each component are the edges of a spanning forest, \f$ n - c \f$ of
 them for \f$ c \f$ components (exercise 12.6); an oriented graph is
 strongly connected if `scc()` gives one component, or if every node is
 reached from a node \f$ r \f$ in \f$ G \f$ and in its transpose (exercise
 12.7); and the distances of `distance()` are the lengths of the shortest
 paths from \f$ r \f$, by Lemma 1 (exercise 12.8). The `dfs2()` of the book
 pushes all the neighbours of a node when it pops it, so that the last
 neighbour pushed is visited first: on \f$ 0 \to 1 \f$, \f$ 0 \to 2 \f$,
 \f$ 1 \to 2 \f$ it visits \f$ 0 , 2 , 1 \f$ where the recursion visits
 \f$ 0 , 1 , 2 \f$; `dfs()` keeps instead on its stack the node and the
 position in its list of neighbours, which visits them in the order of the
 recursion (exercise 12.9). Exercises 12.1 and 12.3 are the drawings of the
 representations and of the visits of figure 12.7.

 \section theory_graph_lemon Interoperation with LEMON

 Beyond the book, the headers of `ADS/interop/lemon` connect ADS with LEMON
 (Dezső, Jüttner and Kovács) in both directions, compiled only by a program
 that includes them with LEMON, `ADS_WITH_LEMON` defined (the target
 `ADS::LEMON` of CMake): a program written on LEMON takes a structure of ADS
 by the named template parameters of its algorithms, with no other change,
 and a program written on ADS runs the algorithms of LEMON on its graphs.
 The algorithms of LEMON take their heap by their traits, with the concept
 `lemon::concepts::Heap`, whose protocol has a part that is not in the
 signatures: the cross-reference map from the items to `int` belongs to the
 caller, `Dijkstra::init()` writes `PRE_HEAP` into it for every node without
 calling the heap, and the heap must therefore derive the state of an item
 from that value alone.

 <em>Lemma 20 (the slots of `DaryHeap`).</em> Let the cross-reference map
 hold, for an item in the heap, its slot \f$ s \ge 0 \f$ in an
 `IndexedDaryHeap` of ADS, and `PRE_HEAP` or `POST_HEAP` otherwise, with the
 freed slots kept in a list and taken again before a new one. Then `state()`
 is right after any sequence of the operations of the concept and of writes
 of `PRE_HEAP` by the caller, and the heap of ADS has at most
 \f$ f \f$ ids, \f$ f \f$ the largest number of items in the heap at once.

 Indeed, `push()` writes the slot into the map before the heap of ADS takes
 it, and `pop()`, `erase()` and `state( i , st )` write `POST_HEAP` (or
 \f$ st \f$) when the slot is freed, so that a nonnegative value is always
 the slot of an item in the heap; a write of the caller sets a negative
 value, which `state()` reads as it is. A new slot is taken only when the
 list is empty, i.e., when every slot is in use, whence the bound. Each
 operation costs that of the heap of ADS plus one read of the map, one
 dependent load more than `BinHeap` makes, whose map holds the place itself;
 removing it needs the place of an item to be kept in the map of LEMON by
 the heap of ADS, a parameter that `IndexedDaryHeap` does not have.

 <em>Lemma 21 (the lazy heap).</em> Let a monotone queue without
 decrease-key hold copies ( slot , key ), a decrease or an increase push a
 new copy, a slot be never reused before `clear()`, and a copy be dropped
 at the top when its slot left or its key is not the priority of the slot.
 If every key pushed is not less than the last key popped, `top()` and
 `prio()` give an item of least priority, and a sequence of \f$ q \f$ pushes
 of copies costs at most \f$ q \f$ extractions.

 Indeed, the live copy of a slot is the last one pushed, whose key is the
 priority of the slot; any other copy of the slot either has another key or
 is a duplicate of the live one, dropped once its slot left. The live copies
 are therefore the items with their priorities, the queue extracts the least
 key first, and the dropped copies precede it with keys not greater, which
 the monotone use allows; each copy is extracted once. Dijkstra's algorithm
 with nonnegative integer lengths satisfies the hypothesis, and pushes each
 node once, so that the slots are at most \f$ n \f$.

 The maps of LEMON are observers of the notifier of their graph, attached at
 their construction under its lock; the `DenseMap` of ADS is an array by the
 ids that attaches to nothing, so that \f$ k \f$ runs of an algorithm of
 LEMON on one graph that does not change share no mutable state and run on
 the threads of an executor of ADS. The `LemonTap` is the converse
 observer: an `ObserverBase` of the notifier whose `add()`, `erase()`,
 `build()` and `clear()` become the events of ADS `Inserted`, `Erased`,
 `Rebuilt` and `Cleared`, the id of the item being the handle, which keeps
 the parallel arcs of LEMON apart. The notifier tells an insertion after it
 and an erasure before it, and it calls its observers from the oldest on
 `add()` and `build()` and from the newest on `erase()` and `clear()`, which
 we read in the code of the notifier and which its header does not state: a
 tap constructed after the map it reads finds the map grown at `add()` and
 the value still there at `erase()`. An insertion is told with the strong
 guarantee of `emit( e , undo )`, so that an observer of ADS that throws
 leaves those before it as they were, and abLEMON removes the item again;
 the erasures cannot fail, since LEMON forbids it. The `LemonMirror` keeps a
 `ListDigraph` in step with a graph of ADS, in \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ expected time
 per event, and records the kinds of the events since the last query, so
 that an algorithm of LEMON with a state of its own knows when it must be
 built again.

 Two solves from scratch are delegated to LEMON when `ADS_WITH_LEMON` is
 defined, by its public interface only. `maxCardinalityMatching()` and
 `maxWeightMatching()` copy the graph into a `SmartGraph` and run
 `MaxMatching` and `MaxWeightedMatching`; the matching of largest weight
 among those of largest size stays with ADS, since LEMON has none. The
 solve from scratch of `Assignment` runs `MinCostAssignment` of abLEMON,
 whose potentials \f$ \pi \f$ satisfy \f$ c( i , j ) \ge \pi( b_j ) - \pi(
 r_i ) \f$ on every edge, with equality on the matching.

 <em>Lemma 22 (the potentials of LEMON).</em> With \f$ u_i = - \pi( r_i ) \f$
 and \f$ v_j = \pi( b_j ) \f$ the reduced costs \f$ c( i , j ) - u_i - v_j \f$
 of the finite costs are nonnegative and zero on the pairs of the
 assignment, i.e., \f$ ( u , v ) \f$ satisfies (11) with the pairs of the
 assignment of LEMON tight, the hypothesis of Lemma 15, from which the
 repairs of ADS go on.

 Indeed, \f$ c( i , j ) - u_i - v_j = c( i , j ) + \pi( r_i ) - \pi( b_j )
 \f$, which is the slack of the inequality of LEMON. The same conversion
 holds for the potentials of `NetworkSimplex` on the network of the
 assignment, whose reduced cost of an arc is \f$ c + \pi( \mathit{tail} )
 - \pi( \mathit{head} ) \f$, and `NetworkSimplexAssignment` gives them to
 `Assignment` in the same way.

 LEMON has no warm start of its minimum cost flows nor of its maximum flow,
 and ADS writes one into private copies of six of its headers, rewritten at
 configure time by the rules of `ADS/interop/lemon/shim/rewrites.txt`, each
 a line `header|anchor|what` whose anchor must occur once in the header,
 and put on the include path before LEMON. `NetworkSimplex` goes on from the
 basis of the last run, `CostScaling` and `CycleCanceling` from its flow
 made feasible again, `CapacityScaling` from its flow and potentials, each
 with setters of one arc or node in \f$ O( 1 ) \f$, and `Preflow` from a
 flow repaired as follows.

 <em>Lemma 23 (the repair of the deficits).</em> Let \f$ f \f$ satisfy the
 capacities, and let the nodes \f$ v \ne s \f$ with more flow out than in
 be repeatedly taken, the flow of an arc out of \f$ v \f$ that carries
 \f$ f( a ) > 0 \f$ lowered by \f$ \min\{ f( a ) , -e( v ) \} \f$,
 \f$ e( v ) \f$ the excess of \f$ v \f$. With integer values the repair
 ends after at most \f$ \sum_a f( a ) \f$ steps, and it leaves a preflow,
 i.e., no node but \f$ s \f$ with a negative excess, of value at least
 that of \f$ f \f$ less the deficits removed.

 Indeed, a node with \f$ e( v ) < 0 \f$ has an arc out with positive
 flow, since its outflow exceeds its inflow \f$ \ge 0 \f$; each step lowers
 the total flow by at least 1, and moves the deficit removed at \f$ v \f$
 to the head of the arc, so that the excesses of the other nodes do not
 fall below 0 but by the step that marks them again. A preflow is what the
 two phases of Goldberg and Tarjan start from, and `Preflow::initWarm()`
 hands it to `init()`. `MaxFlowObserver` takes its solves from an engine,
 the concept `FlowEngine` of `Reoptimization.h`, the augmentations of ADS by
 default and `PreflowEngine` on this warm start; the choice between them
 waits for the measures of the two. A program must see one rewritten
 copy of each header of LEMON, since two layouts of one class in one binary
 violate the one definition rule, which compilers do not diagnose.

